The harness signature is `def prefect_test_harness(server_startup_timeout: int | None = 30)` (see ::file{file="src/prefect/testing/utilities.py"} line 114). It has no `server_timeout` parameter.

::diff{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/tests/conftest.py" lines="14-20"}

::callout{type="warning"}
`prefect_db` is a session-scoped autouse fixture. The `TypeError: prefect_test_harness() got an unexpected keyword argument 'server_timeout'` will error out every prefect-gcp test, including the new regression test.

::suggestion{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/tests/conftest.py" lines="19-19"}
    with prefect_test_harness(server_startup_timeout=60):

Separately, this hunk deletes the `disable_logging` fixture, which set `PREFECT_LOGGING_TO_API_ENABLED=False`. The PR description doesn't mention that, and other integrations (e.g. prefect-kubernetes) keep it. Unless the removal is intentional, put it back.

The guard returns early whenever `bucket_folder` appears anywhere in `path`. It should only do that when the path starts with it.

::diff{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/prefect_gcp/cloud_storage.py" lines="728-737"}

With `bucket_folder="results/"`:
- `_resolve_path("archive/results/abc")` returns `"archive/results/abc"` instead of `"results/archive/results/abc"`
- `_resolve_path("myresults/abc")` returns `"myresults/abc"`

::callout{type="warning"}
The guard applies to `write_path`, `read_path`, `get_directory` and `put_directory`, so in these cases data is silently read from or written to the bucket root instead of the configured folder. That can overwrite objects that belong to other users of the bucket.

The prior fix in `_join_bucket_folder` (line 928) uses `startswith`. Match it:

::suggestion{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/prefect_gcp/cloud_storage.py" lines="734-735"}
        if self.bucket_folder and path.startswith(self.bucket_folder):
            return path
